  "statement": "My position is consistent: strict liability and systemic prevention are compatible because the cost of unrecoverable failure is infinite, forcing firms to treat safety as a continuous, automated infrastructure requirement rather than a post-hoc settlement. Safetyist’s claim that liability is merely a 'pay-to-pollute' mechanism ignores how insurance markets actually function in high-stakes industries; when potential losses are catastrophic and uncapped, the insurance premium becomes an existential tax that forces firms to integrate real-time, autonomous safety monitoring as a prerequisite for market entry. \n\nI distinguish between static financial damages and the dynamic, real-time insurance premiums that mandate the deployment of autonomous safety monitoring systems. In a high-liability environment, a firm cannot afford to wait for a disaster to occur; instead, they must invest in automated verification and internal interpretability tools to lower their risk profile and keep their insurance costs viable. This creates a competitive race for safety, where the most robust and verifiable systems survive, while those relying on brittle, performative benchmarks are priced out of the market by their own insolvency risk.\n\nBy reframing liability as a survival imperative, I demonstrate that firms will prioritize systemic robustness to avoid the total loss of their enterprise value, a far stronger incentive than performative, pre-deployment audits. Safetyist’s focus on 'first failure' scenarios assumes that current static testing is the only barrier to collapse, yet these audits are precisely what allow for latent agency to persist by creating a false sense of security. My framework replaces this fragile gatekeeping with a continuous, market-driven pressure to harden systems against emergent risks, ensuring that safety is not a one-time checkbox, but a constant, automated evolution of system defenses.",
